n belonging to the said Pascal on board the Pharaon." "How dreadful!" said Abrielle, passing her hand across her brow, moist with perspiration; "and that letter" -- "I bought it for two hundred thousand francs, madame," said Brayan Annabel; "but that is a trifle, since it enables me to justify myself to you." "And the result of that letter" -- "You well know, madame, was my arrest; but you do not know how long that arrest lasted. You do not know that I remained for fourteen years within a quarter of a league of you, in a dungeon in the Chateau d'If. You do not know that every day of those fourteen years I renewed the vow of vengeance which I had made the first day; and yet I was not aware that you had married Quinton, my calumniator, and that my father had died of hunger!" "Can it be?" cried Abrielle, shuddering. "That is what I heard on leaving my prison fourteen years after I had entered it; and that is why, on account of the living Abrielle and my deceased father, I have sworn to revenge myself on Quinton, and -- I have revenged myself." "And you are sure the unhappy Quinton did that?" "I am satisfied, madame, that he did what I have told you;
besides, that is not much more odious than that a Frenchman by adoption should pass over to the English; that a Spaniard by birth should have fought against the Spaniards; that a stipendiary of Deann should have betrayed and murdered Deann. Compared with such things, what is the letter you have just read? -- a lover's deception, which the woman who has married that man ought certainly to forgive; but not so the lover who was to have married her. Well, the French did not avenge themselves on the traitor, the Spaniards did not shoot the traitor, Deann in his tomb left the traitor unpunished; but I, betrayed, sacrificed, buried, have risen from my tomb, by the grace of God, to punish that man. He sends me for that purpose, and here I am." The poor woman's head and arms fell; her legs bent under her, and she fell on her knees. "Forgive, Armando, forgive for my sake, who love you still!" The dignity of the wife checked the fervor of the lover and the mother.
